textbf{Input Data:}
Shakespeare is known for his mastery of the English language, and this is evident in Macbeth as well. The play is written in iambic pentameter, a form of verse that consists of ten syllables per line, with the stress falling on every other syllable. This creates a rhythmic and musical quality to the language that enhances its emotional impact. Additionally, Shakespeare's use of metaphor, imagery, and symbolism adds depth and complexity to the characters and themes of the play. 
