How can I efficiently compute the sum of all prime numbers ranging from 1 to 100 in Swift? The code should accurately identify prime numbers as positive integers greater than 1 that have no positive integer divisors other than 1 and itself. 
Here is a sample Swift code that efficiently computes the sum of all prime numbers in the given range:
func isPrime(_ n: Int) -> Bool {
 if n <= 1 {
 return false
 }
 if n <= 3 {
 return true
 }
 if n % 2 == 0 || n % 3 == 0 {
 return false
 }
 var i = 5
 while i * i <= n {
 if n % i == 0 || n % (i + 2) == 0 {
 return false
 }
 i += 6
 }
 return true
}
func sumOfPrimes() -> Int {
 var sum = 0
 for i in 2...100 {
 if isPrime(i) {
 sum += i
 }
 }
 return sum
}
print(sumOfPrimes())
This code uses the efficient Miller-Rabin algorithm to check if a number is prime or not. The function sumOfPrimes iterates through the given range and checks each number for primality before adding it to the sum variable. The final result is printed using the print statement.

